/* Days 12 to 14 */
(window.DAYS = window.DAYS || []).push(
{
  n: 12, title: 'Frontend II: state, data, security', tagline: 'Where state lives, how data gets to the screen, how real-time and offline work, and how to not get hacked.',
  goals: ['Separate UI state, server state, URL state and form state, and choose a tool for each', 'Design data fetching with caching, pagination, optimistic updates and race protection', 'Handle live updates and offline gracefully', 'Defend against XSS, CSRF and common client-side mistakes'],
  plan: [{ min: 20, what: 'Lesson 1: state management', to: 'l0' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 2: data fetching and the network', to: 'l1' }, { min: 15, what: 'Lesson 3: real-time and offline', to: 'l2' }, { min: 20, what: 'Lesson 4: web security', to: 'l3' }, { min: 10, what: 'Lab: debounce and stale responses', to: 'lab' }, { min: 10, what: 'Quiz', to: 'quiz' }, { min: 20, what: 'Practice: live order tracking page', to: 'practice' }],
  lessons: [
    {
      title: 'State management', short: 'State', min: 20, body: [
        "**The problem:** UIs are functions of state, and bugs are almost always state that is out of sync, duplicated or in the wrong place. The core skill is deciding what is state, who owns it and where it lives.",
        { h: 'Kinds of state, each with a natural home' },
        { table: { head: ['Kind', 'Example', 'Where it lives'], rows: [['Local UI state', 'Is the dropdown open? Text in an input', 'Component state (`useState`). Keep it as local as possible'], ['Shared UI state', 'Selected tab used by several components, theme, modal stack', 'Lift to the nearest common parent, context, or a small store'], ['Server state', 'Products, user profile, feed: data owned by the backend and cached on the client', 'A server-state cache (React Query, SWR, Apollo): handles caching, refetching, dedupe, loading and error states'], ['URL state', 'Filters, sort, page, selected item', 'The URL (query string): shareable, back-button friendly, survives refresh'], ['Form state', 'Inputs, validation, dirty/touched', 'A form library or local state; validate on blur/submit'], ['Persistent client state', 'Draft text, preferences', 'localStorage or IndexedDB']] } },
        { h: 'Principles' },
        { ul: ['**Single source of truth.** Store each fact once. Duplicated state drifts.', '**Derive, do not store.** If you can compute it from other state (a filtered list, a total), compute it (memoize if expensive).', '**Colocate.** Put state as close as possible to where it is used; lift only when needed.', '**Normalize entities.** Store `postsById` and a list of ids instead of nested copies, so updating one post updates it everywhere.', '**Unidirectional flow.** Actions go up, state flows down. Flux/Redux formalize it: dispatch an action, a pure reducer produces new state, the UI re-renders. Predictable and debuggable, but verbose; lighter stores (Zustand, Jotai, signals) give the same discipline with less ceremony.'] },
        { warn: "Copying server data into a global store by hand and then writing code to keep it fresh. That is re-implementing a cache badly. Use a server-state library and keep the global store for genuine client-only state." },
        { say: "I will keep server data in a query cache keyed by request, filters in the URL, ephemeral UI state local to components, and only a small global store for things like the current user and theme." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Data fetching and the network', short: 'Data fetching', min: 25, body: [
        "A request has more states than 'done': idle, loading, success, error, and **stale** (shown, but refreshing). Designing all of them, not just success, is what separates polished UIs.",
        { h: 'Caching and freshness' },
        "Cache responses by key (URL + params). Apply **stale-while-revalidate**: show cached data immediately and refetch in the background, updating when it arrives. Deduplicate identical in-flight requests, refetch on window focus or reconnect, set a staleness time per data type (profile: minutes; stock price: seconds), and invalidate related keys after a mutation.",
        { h: 'Pagination and long lists' },
        { ul: ['Use **cursor pagination** from the API (Day 2). **Infinite scroll** observes a sentinel element with `IntersectionObserver` and fetches the next page when it nears the viewport; also offer a visible "load more" fallback for accessibility.', '**Virtualize** (windowing) when the list can grow beyond a few hundred items: render only visible rows plus a small overscan, and keep the scroll height with spacer elements.', 'Preserve **scroll position** when navigating back, and keep list data cached so back is instant.'] },
        { h: 'Optimistic updates' },
        "Update the UI immediately and reconcile when the server answers, rolling back on failure. It makes cheap, usually successful actions (like, toggle, reorder) feel instant.",
        { diagram: 'optimistic-ui' },
        { h: 'Race conditions' },
        "Requests return in any order. A slow response for an old query can overwrite a newer one. Defend with **AbortController** (cancel the previous request), or compare a request id/counter and ignore stale responses, plus debouncing. The lab shows the bug.",
        { h: 'Other things to mention' },
        { ul: ['**Request waterfalls:** a parent fetches, renders, then the child fetches. Hoist fetches or start them in parallel; prefetch on hover or route intent.', '**Batching:** combine many small requests (GraphQL, batch endpoints, `dataloader`) to cut round trips.', '**Retries** with backoff for idempotent GETs; surface errors with a retry button; use error boundaries so one failed widget does not blank the page.', '**Skeletons** that match the final layout reduce perceived wait and layout shift.', '**Idempotency keys** on mutations so retries are safe.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Real-time updates and offline', short: 'Real-time and offline', min: 15, body: [
        { h: 'Live data on the client' },
        { ul: ['Choose transport by direction and frequency (Day 2): SSE for server-to-client streams, WebSocket for two-way, polling for rare changes.', '**Reconnect** automatically with exponential backoff and jitter. Detect dead connections with heartbeats. On reconnect, **resume** by sending the last event id you saw, or refetch to resync, since you missed events while offline.', '**Merge** live events into the same cache the UI reads from (update the query cache entry) so there is one source of truth, not a parallel live state.', 'Handle **duplicates and ordering** by event id or version; ignore events older than what you hold.', 'Throttle UI updates for high-frequency streams (batch into one render per animation frame).'] },
        { h: 'Offline-first' },
        { ol: ['Cache the **app shell** and assets with a service worker so the app opens offline.', 'Read from a local store (IndexedDB) first, then sync.', 'Record user actions as a **queue of pending mutations** (with client-generated ids and idempotency keys) and replay them in order when the network returns; show a "pending" state.', 'Resolve **conflicts** (the server changed the item too): last-write-wins for simple fields, field-level merge, or prompt the user. For collaborative content, CRDTs (Day 5, Day 13).', 'Sync state across tabs with `BroadcastChannel` or storage events.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Web security essentials', short: 'Security', min: 20, body: [
        "The browser runs untrusted code from many origins side by side, so most defenses are about isolating origins and never mixing data with code.",
        { table: { head: ['Threat', 'What it is', 'Defense'], rows: [['XSS (cross-site scripting)', 'Attacker gets their script to run in your page (through unescaped user content, a vulnerable dependency, injected HTML), then reads tokens and acts as the user', 'Escape output (frameworks do by default; avoid `innerHTML` and `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`), sanitize any HTML you must render (DOMPurify), a strict **Content Security Policy**, `HttpOnly` cookies so scripts cannot read them'], ['CSRF (cross-site request forgery)', 'Another site makes the browser send your authenticated cookie to your server (a hidden form post)', '`SameSite=Lax/Strict` cookies, CSRF tokens on state-changing requests, check `Origin`. Does not apply to tokens sent manually in headers'], ['CORS misunderstandings', 'The same-origin policy blocks a page from reading responses from other origins. CORS is how a *server* opts specific origins in', 'Allow only needed origins; never `*` with credentials. CORS protects users\' browsers, not your API: servers must still authenticate every request'], ['Clickjacking', 'Your site framed invisibly by an attacker', '`Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors` or `X-Frame-Options`'], ['Token theft / storage', 'Tokens in `localStorage` are readable by XSS', 'Prefer `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite` cookies for session tokens; if using bearer tokens keep them short-lived in memory with refresh cookies'], ['Supply chain', 'A compromised npm package runs in your page', 'Lockfiles, audits, minimal dependencies, subresource integrity for CDN scripts, CSP'], ['Leaked secrets', 'API keys inside the bundle are public', 'Never ship secrets to the client; proxy through your backend']] } },
        { ul: ['Always use **HTTPS** and HSTS. Validate input on the server too: client validation is a convenience, not security.', 'Authorization is enforced on the server for every request; hiding a button is not access control.', 'Third-party scripts have the full power of your page: load them sandboxed (iframes) or sparingly.'] },
        { tip: "Mentioning XSS, CSRF and token storage in 30 seconds when you design any login or user-generated-content feature is a cheap way to show breadth." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Accessibility and internationalization', short: 'A11y and i18n', deep: true, body: [
        { h: 'Accessibility (a11y)' },
        { ul: ['**Semantic HTML first:** `button`, `a`, `label`, `nav`, `main`, headings in order. A `div` with a click handler gives screen readers and keyboards nothing.', '**Keyboard:** everything reachable and operable with Tab/Enter/Space/Arrows/Escape, visible focus ring, logical focus order, **focus management** in modals (trap focus, return it on close), no keyboard traps.', '**ARIA** only to fill gaps semantic HTML cannot (`role`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-live` for announcing updates). Wrong ARIA is worse than none.', '**Labels and alt text** for inputs and images; **colour contrast** at least 4.5:1 for body text; do not rely on colour alone.', 'Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; support zoom to 200%; touch targets of about 44 px.', 'Test with a keyboard, a screen reader and automated tools (axe), which catch only a portion of issues.'] },
        { h: 'Internationalization (i18n)' },
        { ul: ['Never concatenate translated strings; use message templates with placeholders and **plural rules** (ICU MessageFormat), since languages have 1 to 6 plural forms.', 'Use `Intl` APIs for dates, numbers, currency, relative time, sorting.', 'Support **right-to-left** layouts with CSS logical properties (`margin-inline-start`) and `dir`.', 'Allow text expansion (German is 30% longer), avoid text in images, and load locale bundles lazily.', 'Time zones: store UTC, display local; locale is not the same as language or country.'] }
      ]
    }
  ],
  lab: { title: 'Race conditions in search-as-you-type', min: 10, body: ["Type \"react\" fast with and without debounce, and with and without the stale-response check. Watch which response ends up on screen.", { widget: 'debounce' }, { ul: ['Turn debounce off and the stale check off, then type fast several times. Can the final result be for an older query?', 'Turn the stale check on. What does the log show for older responses?', 'Which is more important for correctness, debounce or the stale check? Why?'] }] },
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Where should list filters and the current page typically live so users can share a link and use the back button?', opts: ['A global Redux store', 'The URL query string', 'localStorage', 'A cookie'], a: 1, why: 'URL state is shareable, bookmarkable and works with browser history.' },
    { q: 'Search results for "re" arrive after results for "react" and overwrite them. How do you fix it?', opts: ['Increase the timeout', 'Cancel previous requests or ignore responses that are not for the latest query', 'Use a bigger server', 'Disable caching'], a: 1, why: 'Responses can reorder; make the latest intent win using AbortController or request ids.' },
    { q: 'Which defence stops a malicious script from reading your session cookie?', opts: ['SameSite', 'HttpOnly', 'CORS', 'HTTPS'], a: 1, why: 'HttpOnly hides the cookie from JavaScript. SameSite is aimed at CSRF.' },
    { q: 'When is an optimistic update a poor choice?', opts: ['Toggling a like', 'A payment confirmation that frequently fails or cannot be undone', 'Reordering a list', 'Marking a task done'], a: 1, why: 'Optimism suits cheap, reversible, usually successful actions. Not irreversible or often-failing ones.' },
    { q: 'What is the problem with storing a copy of server data in your own global store?', opts: ['It is faster', 'You must hand-build caching, invalidation and refetch logic, and it drifts from the server', 'It is insecure', 'Browsers forbid it'], a: 1, why: 'A server-state library already solves staleness, dedupe and refetching. Keep the global store for client-only state.' },
    { q: 'A CORS configuration allows `*`. What does it NOT do?', opts: ['Let any origin read public responses', 'Replace authentication: your API must still check credentials on every request', 'Affect browsers', 'Apply to preflight'], a: 1, why: 'CORS is a browser-enforced read restriction, not an access control mechanism for your server.' }
  ],
  practice: {
    min: 20,
    prompt: ["**Live order tracking page** (like a food delivery app). The user sees the order status (confirmed, preparing, on the way, delivered), an ETA, and the courier's position on a map updating every few seconds. The app must survive flaky mobile networks and the user switching to another app and back.", "Describe the client architecture: what state exists and where it lives, how data arrives (initial load plus live updates), how you handle reconnects, errors and offline, and what you do for accessibility and performance."],
    hints: ['Initial load and live updates should end up in the same place.', 'What if the user was offline for 2 minutes and missed events?', 'What does a screen reader user need when the status changes?'],
    outline: [
      { ul: ['**Data:** order and courier info are server state in a query cache keyed by `order/{id}`. Initial fetch via HTTP (SSR optional, but this page is private, so CSR with a skeleton is fine).', '**Live updates:** SSE or WebSocket channel per order; events carry a monotonically increasing sequence id. Events update the query cache entry; ignore stale sequence ids. Location updates are throttled to one render per frame and the marker is animated with `transform`.', '**Reconnect:** exponential backoff with jitter; on reconnect and on tab becoming visible, refetch the order snapshot (resync) and resume the stream from the last sequence id. Fall back to polling every 10 s if the socket cannot connect.', '**Offline and errors:** show the last known state with a "last updated" time and an offline banner; retry button; error boundary around the map so a map failure does not hide the status.', '**UI state:** local state for map zoom and panel open; selected order id in the URL.', '**Accessibility:** the status is a text region with `aria-live="polite"` so changes are announced; the map is supplemental with a text description of the courier distance and ETA; keyboard operable controls; respect reduced motion.', '**Performance:** lazy-load the map library after the status renders (status first), preconnect to tile servers, keep bundle small, battery friendly update rate when the tab is hidden.'] }
    ],
    rubric: 'Strong answers: separate server state from UI state; merge live events into the cache with sequence ids; reconnect with backoff/jitter and resync on resume or focus; polling fallback; offline/last-updated and error boundaries; throttled map updates; aria-live status; lazy-load map; privacy of live location and auth on the stream.'
  },
  cheat: ['State kinds: local UI, shared UI, server state (query cache), URL state, form state. Single source of truth; derive, colocate, normalize.', 'Fetching: cache + stale-while-revalidate, dedupe, cursor pagination, virtualization, optimistic updates with rollback, abort stale requests, avoid waterfalls.', 'Live data: reconnect with backoff, resume by last id or refetch, merge into the cache, ignore old versions.', 'Offline: service worker for shell, IndexedDB, queue of mutations with idempotency keys, conflict policy.', 'Security: escape output, CSP, HttpOnly + SameSite cookies, CSRF tokens, CORS is not auth, never ship secrets.', 'A11y: semantic HTML, keyboard, focus management, contrast, aria-live. i18n: ICU messages, Intl, logical CSS.'],
  cards: [
    ['List the kinds of frontend state and where each lives.', 'Local UI (component), shared UI (lifted/context/store), server state (query cache), URL state (query string), form state, persistent state (storage).'],
    ['Why not copy server data into Redux by hand?', 'You re-implement caching, invalidation and refetching; use a server-state cache and keep global store for client-only state.'],
    ['What is stale-while-revalidate on the client?', 'Show cached data immediately, refetch in the background, update when fresh data arrives.'],
    ['How do you prevent stale responses overwriting new ones?', 'AbortController to cancel the old request, or ignore responses with an outdated request id; debounce input.'],
    ['What is an optimistic update?', 'Update the UI immediately, send the request, reconcile on success, roll back on failure.'],
    ['How do you implement infinite scroll?', 'Cursor pagination plus an IntersectionObserver on a sentinel; virtualize long lists; keep a fallback button.'],
    ['How should a live client recover after a reconnect?', 'Backoff with jitter; resume from last event id or refetch a snapshot; merge into the cache; dedupe by id/version.'],
    ['XSS vs CSRF?', 'XSS: attacker script runs in your page (defend: escaping, CSP, HttpOnly). CSRF: another site triggers authenticated requests (defend: SameSite, CSRF tokens).'],
    ['What does CORS actually protect?', 'It lets servers relax the browser same-origin read restriction for chosen origins; it is not server-side access control.'],
    ['Three accessibility basics?', 'Semantic HTML, full keyboard operation with visible focus, labels and sufficient contrast (plus aria-live for dynamic updates).']
  ]
},

{
  n: 13, title: 'Frontend designs', tagline: 'A framework for frontend design interviews, then autocomplete, feeds, chat and collaborative editing.',
  goals: ['Run a frontend design interview with the RADIO framework', 'Design autocomplete with correct behaviour, accessibility and performance', 'Design an infinite feed with virtualization and stable scrolling', 'Explain chat UI details and how collaborative editing resolves conflicts'],
  plan: [{ min: 15, what: 'Lesson 1: the RADIO framework', to: 'l0' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 2: autocomplete', to: 'l1' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 3: news feed UI', to: 'l2' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 4: chat UI and collaborative editing', to: 'l3' }, { min: 10, what: 'Quiz', to: 'quiz' }, { min: 20, what: 'Practice: Kanban board', to: 'practice' }],
  lessons: [
    {
      title: 'The RADIO framework', short: 'RADIO', min: 15, body: [
        "Frontend design interviews ask you to design a UI product or component (autocomplete, a feed, a photo gallery, a spreadsheet) and focus on **architecture, data flow, API between components, performance and user experience**, rather than servers. A framework keeps you from only talking about React.",
        { table: { head: ['Letter', 'Step', 'What to cover'], rows: [['R', 'Requirements', 'Features in and out of scope, devices (mobile, desktop), browsers, accessibility, i18n, scale (how many items?), network conditions, SEO, offline'], ['A', 'Architecture', 'Component tree, where state lives, rendering strategy (CSR/SSR/SSG), main modules and how they talk, third-party boundaries'], ['D', 'Data model', 'Entities and their shape, normalization, client state vs server state, caching, how updates flow'], ['I', 'Interface', 'The API between client and server (endpoints, pagination, errors, real-time) and the public props/events of the key components'], ['O', 'Optimizations', 'Performance (bundle, virtualization, lazy loading), accessibility, security, error and loading states, offline, analytics, testing']] } },
        { say: "Before designing, I would like to clarify scope: is this mobile web, desktop or both? How many items might appear? Do we need keyboard and screen reader support? Can results be slightly stale?" },
        { tip: "Draw the component tree and annotate each node with the state it owns. Interviewers read your whiteboard as your mental model." },
        "Spend roughly 5 minutes on requirements, 10 on architecture and data model, 10 on the interface, and the rest on a deep dive plus optimizations. Pick a deep dive where the interviewer shows interest (often accessibility, performance or data flow)."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Design autocomplete (typeahead)', short: 'Autocomplete', min: 25, body: [
        "A deceptively rich question: it exercises networking, state, race conditions, keyboard handling, accessibility and performance in one small component.",
        { h: 'Requirements' },
        "Input with a dropdown of suggestions as the user types; selecting fills the input; keyboard navigation; works on mobile; fast (suggestions under 100 ms perceived); accessible; handles slow or failed network; the suggestion source might be a server or a local list.",
        { diagram: 'typeahead' },
        { h: 'Architecture and state' },
        { ul: ['Components: `Autocomplete` (owns state) containing `Input`, `Dropdown`, `Option`. Make the data source pluggable (a function `fetchSuggestions(query, signal)`) so it works for any backend.', 'State: `query`, `results`, `status` (idle, loading, error), `isOpen`, `highlightedIndex`. Derived: whether to show the "no results" row.'] },
        { h: 'Behaviour that matters' },
        { ul: ['**Debounce** input (150 to 300 ms) and require a minimum length (1 to 2 chars) to avoid useless requests.', '**Cancel or ignore stale responses** with `AbortController` or request ids, so the dropdown always matches the latest query.', '**Cache** results by prefix in memory (a `Map`), including for backspacing; optionally prefetch on focus. For local lists, filter instantly in memory, using a trie for large lists.', '**Keyboard:** Down/Up move highlight (wrap around), Enter selects, Escape closes, Tab leaves, Home/End optionally. Mouse hover must not fight keyboard highlight.', '**Highlight the matching prefix** safely: split the string and wrap with an element; never build HTML from user text (XSS).', '**Empty, loading and error states** inside the dropdown; keep the old results visible while loading to avoid flicker.', '**Mobile:** large touch targets, dismiss on blur, avoid zoom on focus (font size 16px).'] },
        { h: 'Accessibility: the combobox pattern' },
        { code: "<input role=\"combobox\" aria-expanded=\"true\" aria-controls=\"listbox-1\"\n       aria-autocomplete=\"list\" aria-activedescendant=\"opt-3\" />\n<ul role=\"listbox\" id=\"listbox-1\">\n  <li role=\"option\" id=\"opt-3\" aria-selected=\"true\">...</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- plus a polite live region: '5 results available' -->" },
        "Focus stays in the input; `aria-activedescendant` points to the highlighted option so screen readers announce it.",
        { h: 'Server side and optimizations' },
        "The API returns the top 5 to 10 matches for the prefix, ranked by popularity and computed from a precomputed prefix index (Day 10). Cache popular prefixes at the CDN with a short TTL. On the client keep payloads tiny (ids and display text). Log selections to improve ranking."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Design a news feed UI', short: 'Feed UI', min: 25, body: [
        "**Requirements:** an endless vertical list of posts (text, images, video), loads more as you scroll, like and comment actions, new posts appear, smooth on mobile, works for screen readers, fast first paint.",
        { h: 'Data model' },
        "Normalize: `postsById`, `feedIds` (ordered list per feed), `usersById`. Interactions update the single post entity and every place that shows it updates. The server returns pages with a **cursor**, never page numbers.",
        { h: 'Rendering and loading' },
        { ul: ['**First paint:** server-render (or stream) the first page of text and the first image so LCP is fast; hydrate afterwards. Reserve image dimensions via aspect ratio to avoid layout shift.', '**Infinite scroll:** an `IntersectionObserver` on a sentinel triggers fetching the next page when it is about 1 to 2 screens away, with a guard so only one request is in flight. Show a skeleton at the end, and an inline retry on failure.', '**Virtualization:** unmounting posts that are far off screen keeps the DOM small and scrolling smooth. Posts have variable heights, so measure after render and cache heights; keep scroll position stable when items above change height (scroll anchoring).', '**Media:** lazy-load images (`loading="lazy"`, but eager for the first one), use responsive sizes and modern formats; autoplay video only when mostly in view and muted, pause when off screen.'] },
        { h: 'Live content without ruining the reader\'s place' },
        "Never insert new posts above what the user is reading, which makes content jump. Show a floating \"12 new posts\" pill that, when clicked, scrolls to the top and merges them. Refresh stale data when the tab regains focus.",
        { h: 'Interactions' },
        { ul: ['**Like** is an optimistic update with rollback (Day 12), debounced if tapped repeatedly, sent with an idempotency key.', '**Comments** load on demand and paginate separately; compose box keeps drafts in local storage.', '**Scroll restoration** when returning from a post detail: keep the feed cached and restore offset.'] },
        { h: 'Accessibility, performance, errors' },
        { ul: ['Use the `feed` and `article` roles (or semantic `article` elements), headings per post, keyboard shortcuts to move between posts, focus management when new items load, and a visible alternative to infinite scroll for keyboard users ("Load more").', 'Performance budgets, code-split the composer and comment UI, prefetch the next page during idle time.', 'Offline: show cached feed with an offline banner.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Chat UI and collaborative editing', short: 'Chat and collaboration', min: 25, body: [
        { h: 'Chat UI' },
        { ul: ['**Message list** grows at the bottom and loads *older* messages when scrolling up (reverse infinite scroll). When prepending older messages, preserve the user\'s scroll anchor so the view does not jump. If the user is at the bottom, new messages auto-scroll; if they are reading history, show a "new messages" button instead.', '**Optimistic send:** create the message locally with a **client-generated id** and status `sending`, render it immediately, and update to `sent`, `delivered` or `failed` (with retry) based on acks from the server. The server echoes the same client id so you do not show a duplicate.', '**Ordering:** display by server-assigned sequence or timestamp; reconcile local pending messages at the end.', '**Real-time:** WebSocket with reconnect and resume (last seen id); typing indicators are throttled (send at most every few seconds) and expire; read receipts batch.', '**Virtualize** long histories; handle images with reserved sizes; show upload progress with cancel; draft saved per conversation; accessibility via `aria-live` for incoming messages (politely).'] },
        { h: 'Collaborative editing (Google Docs style)' },
        "Many users edit the same document at once. Naive 'last save wins' loses edits. Two families of algorithms keep everyone's copies converging:",
        { table: { head: ['', 'Operational Transformation (OT)', 'CRDT'], rows: [['Idea', 'Edits are operations (insert "x" at position 5). The server transforms concurrent operations against each other so they apply consistently', 'Each character/element has a unique id and position metadata so operations commute; any order of delivery gives the same result'], ['Needs', 'A central server to order operations', 'No central ordering; works peer to peer and offline'], ['Cost', 'Hard to implement correctly', 'Metadata overhead; harder garbage collection'], ['Used by', 'Google Docs', 'Figma-like tools, Yjs, Automerge']] } },
        { ul: ['**Local echo:** apply your own edit instantly, send it to the server, and rebase pending local edits when remote ones arrive.', '**Presence:** colored remote cursors and selections sent as lightweight ephemeral messages (not persisted), throttled.', '**Undo** applies to your own operations only, not to others\'.', '**Persistence:** periodic snapshots plus an operation log; version history for restore; autosave indicators.', '**Offline:** queue operations locally and merge on reconnect (natural with CRDTs).', '**Performance:** a large document needs a virtualized view (render only visible pages) and incremental layout.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Design systems, component APIs and micro-frontends', short: 'Component architecture', deep: true, body: [
        { h: 'Component API design' },
        { ul: ['**Composition over configuration.** A card that takes a dozen boolean props becomes unmanageable; prefer `<Card><Card.Header/><Card.Body/></Card>` or slots and `children`.', '**Controlled vs uncontrolled:** support both (`value`/`onChange` or internal state with `defaultValue`).', '**Headless components** (logic and accessibility without styles) separate behaviour from presentation, so teams can theme freely.', '**Design tokens** (colour, spacing, type scale) as variables shared across platforms; theming by swapping token sets.', '**Accessibility built in**, documented usage, visual regression tests, semantic versioning with a deprecation policy.', 'Keep the public API small, since every prop is a promise you must keep.'] },
        { h: 'Micro-frontends' },
        "Split a large web app into independently built and deployed pieces owned by different teams (checkout, search, account), composed in the browser (Module Federation, web components, iframes) or at the edge/server. **Wins:** independent deploys, team autonomy, incremental migration. **Costs:** duplicate dependencies and larger bundles, inconsistent UX without a shared design system, cross-app communication and routing complexity, harder performance and debugging. Use them when organizational scale demands it, not for a small team."
      ]
    }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'What does the "I" in RADIO stand for?', opts: ['Implementation', 'Interface definition (component API and network API)', 'Infrastructure', 'Iteration'], a: 1, why: 'RADIO: Requirements, Architecture, Data model, Interface, Optimizations.' },
    { q: 'In an accessible autocomplete, where does DOM focus stay while the user arrows through options?', opts: ['On each option', 'On the input, with aria-activedescendant pointing at the highlighted option', 'On the body', 'On the dropdown container'], a: 1, why: 'The combobox pattern keeps typing possible and lets screen readers announce the active option via aria-activedescendant.' },
    { q: 'New posts arrive while the user is reading mid-feed. Best UX?', opts: ['Insert them at the top immediately', 'Show a "new posts" pill that the user taps to merge them', 'Reload the page', 'Ignore new posts'], a: 1, why: 'Inserting above the viewport makes content jump. Let the user decide when to merge.' },
    { q: 'When loading older chat messages at the top, what must you preserve?', opts: ['Nothing', 'The scroll anchor so the visible messages do not jump', 'The websocket', 'The cache'], a: 1, why: 'Prepending content shifts everything down; adjust scroll offset by the added height.' },
    { q: 'Why give each optimistic chat message a client-generated id?', opts: ['For encryption', 'So the server\'s echo can be matched to the pending message instead of showing a duplicate', 'To sort alphabetically', 'To save bandwidth'], a: 1, why: 'The client id links the local pending message to its confirmed version and also acts as an idempotency key.' },
    { q: 'Main advantage of CRDTs over OT for collaboration?', opts: ['They are simpler to build', 'They converge without a central server ordering operations and support offline editing naturally', 'They use less memory', 'They avoid cursors'], a: 1, why: 'CRDT operations commute, so replicas converge regardless of delivery order; OT needs a central transformation server.' }
  ],
  practice: {
    min: 20,
    prompt: ["**Design a Kanban board (like Trello) for the web.** Boards have columns and cards. Users drag cards between columns and reorder them. Several people can have the same board open and see each other's changes live. It should work with a spotty connection and handle boards with 2,000 cards.", "Use RADIO: requirements, component architecture, data model, API and real-time protocol, and optimizations. Pay special attention to drag and drop, ordering, conflicts and performance."],
    hints: ['How do you store the order of cards so reordering is cheap and conflict-friendly?', 'What happens if two people move the same card at once?', 'How do you render 2,000 cards smoothly?'],
    outline: [
      { ul: ['**Requirements:** CRUD cards and columns, drag and drop (mouse, touch and keyboard), real-time collaboration, offline tolerance, up to 2,000 cards; accessibility for drag and drop through keyboard commands and announcements.', '**Architecture:** Board, Column, Card components; a store (normalized) holds `cardsById`, `columnsById`, and each column\'s ordered `cardIds`; a sync layer manages the socket and the mutation queue; the DnD layer is separate and emits `move(cardId, toColumn, index)`.', '**Ordering:** use fractional indexing (a position string/number between neighbours, e.g. 1.5 between 1 and 2) so moving one card changes one value, not the whole list; periodically rebalance. This also merges well when two users reorder different cards.', '**Mutations and conflicts:** each move is an operation sent with a client id and a base version, applied optimistically. Server applies in order; concurrent moves of the same card resolve by last-write-wins on that card (acceptable), broadcasting the final state; clients reconcile. Offline operations are queued in IndexedDB and replayed idempotently.', '**Real-time:** WebSocket per board; events for card updates with sequence numbers; on reconnect, resume from last sequence or refetch the board; presence shows who is viewing and drags in progress (ephemeral).', '**Performance:** virtualize cards inside columns (and columns horizontally if many); memoize `Card` by id so a move re-renders two columns, not the board; lightweight drag preview; lazy-load card detail modal; paginate or lazy-load card details for huge boards.', '**Accessibility:** keyboard drag (space to pick up, arrows to move, space to drop) with `aria-live` announcements; focus returns to the moved card.'] }
    ],
    rubric: 'Strong answers use RADIO, a normalized store with ordered id lists, fractional indexing for ordering, optimistic moves with versions and a conflict policy, WebSocket with sequence ids and resync, offline queue, virtualization and memoization for 2,000 cards, and keyboard accessible drag and drop with announcements.'
  },
  cheat: ['RADIO: Requirements, Architecture, Data model, Interface, Optimizations.', 'Autocomplete: debounce, cancel stale, cache by prefix, keyboard, combobox ARIA, safe highlighting, empty/loading/error states.', 'Feed UI: normalized store, cursor, IntersectionObserver, virtualization, no content jumps, optimistic like, aria feed.', 'Chat UI: reverse infinite scroll with scroll anchoring, client ids and optimistic send, resume by last id, throttled typing.', 'Collab editing: OT (central server transforms) vs CRDT (commutative, offline); local echo, presence, per-user undo.', 'Fractional indexing makes reordering cheap and mergeable.'],
  cards: [
    ['Expand RADIO.', 'Requirements, Architecture, Data model, Interface, Optimizations.'],
    ['List the behaviours an autocomplete needs.', 'Debounce, min length, cancel/ignore stale responses, prefix cache, keyboard navigation, safe match highlighting, loading/empty/error states, combobox ARIA.'],
    ['Which ARIA attributes make a combobox accessible?', 'role=combobox on input, aria-expanded, aria-controls, aria-autocomplete, aria-activedescendant; role=listbox/option for the list.'],
    ['How do you avoid layout jumps in a live feed?', 'Do not insert above the viewport; show a "new posts" pill; reserve image sizes; scroll anchoring.'],
    ['Why virtualize a list?', 'Rendering only visible rows keeps DOM size and work small so scroll and interactions stay fast.'],
    ['What is reverse infinite scroll?', 'Chat loads older messages when scrolling up and adjusts scroll offset to keep the visible messages in place.'],
    ['How does optimistic send work in chat?', 'Render with a client id and sending status immediately; update on server ack/echo; show failed with retry.'],
    ['OT vs CRDT?', 'OT transforms concurrent operations via a central server; CRDTs use commutative operations with unique ids so replicas converge without central ordering and work offline.'],
    ['What is fractional indexing?', 'Assigning each item a position between its neighbours so one move changes one value.'],
    ['Name two micro-frontend costs.', 'Duplicate dependencies/larger bundles; inconsistent UX and integration complexity.']
  ]
},

{
  n: 14, title: 'Mock interviews and mastery', tagline: 'Put it all together under time pressure, then build the habits that keep the knowledge for years.',
  goals: ['Run a full 45-minute design with a clear structure and timing', 'Recognize the signals that separate senior answers from junior ones', 'Apply the method to harder designs: ticketing, payments, ride hailing, editors', 'Have a personal plan for retaining and deepening everything after today'],
  plan: [{ min: 20, what: 'Lesson 1: the interview playbook', to: 'l0' }, { min: 15, what: 'Lesson 2: the master checklist', to: 'l1' }, { min: 20, what: 'Lesson 3: four harder designs, outlined', to: 'l2' }, { min: 45, what: 'Timed mock interview (use the timer)', to: 'lab' }, { min: 10, what: 'Quiz: mixed review', to: 'quiz' }, { min: 10, what: 'Practice: teach it back', to: 'practice' }],
  lessons: [
    {
      title: 'The interview playbook', short: 'Playbook', min: 20, body: [
        { h: 'Behaviours that interviewers reward' },
        { ul: ['**Drive the conversation.** You lead: state the plan, check in ("does this scope sound right?"), and move on at the right time. Do not wait to be asked.', '**Think out loud.** Silence looks like being stuck. Narrate options and why you pick one.', '**Start simple, then evolve.** Draw the simplest design that meets the requirements, then say "this breaks at X, so I add Y". It shows you add complexity for reasons.', '**Quantify.** Numbers turn opinions into engineering.', '**Name trade-offs and the alternative you rejected.** There is no best option, only best for these requirements.', '**Talk failure.** What happens when this node dies, the network splits, the cache is cold, traffic doubles?', '**Be honest about the unknown.** "I have not used X, but I would expect it to behave like Y, and I would verify Z" beats bluffing.'] },
        { h: 'When things go wrong' },
        { table: { head: ['Situation', 'What to do'], rows: [['You are stuck', 'Go back to requirements and numbers; say what you know; pick the simplest option and note the risk'], ['Interviewer pushes back', 'It is information, not an attack. Restate their concern, evaluate it honestly, adjust the design or defend with a reason'], ['You made a wrong assumption or number', 'Say so immediately, fix it and carry the consequence forward. Self-correction is a strong signal'], ['Running out of time', 'Say what you would cover next, then summarize the design in 30 seconds'], ['Interviewer wants depth somewhere new', 'Follow them: they are telling you what they care about']] } },
        { h: 'Senior signals' },
        { ul: ['You ask about **business goals and constraints** (cost, team size, time to market), not only technical ones.', 'You plan **evolution**: what to build first (v1), what to add at 10x and 100x.', 'You consider **operations**: deployment, monitoring, on-call, migrations, data privacy and security.', 'You design for **failure and recovery**, and test assumptions: load tests, canaries, chaos experiments.', 'You know **when not to build**: use a managed service, keep one database, delay sharding.'] },
        { warn: "Buzzword stacking: 'Kafka, Redis, Cassandra, Kubernetes' with no reason attached. Every box must answer 'what problem does this remove, and what does it cost?'" },
        { h: 'Whiteboard hygiene' },
        "Top-left: requirements and out of scope. Corner: key numbers. Centre: the diagram, left to right in request order, with data stores at the right. Number the flows (1, 2, 3) when you walk through a request. Leave room for the deep dive."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'The master checklist', short: 'Checklist', min: 15, body: [
        "Run this list mentally for any design. It is the whole course compressed into questions.",
        { table: { head: ['Area', 'Question to answer', 'Typical tools'], rows: [['Requirements', 'What must it do, how well, for whom, what is out of scope?', 'Functional and non-functional lists'], ['Scale', 'QPS, read:write, data size, growth, peak, geography?', 'Back-of-envelope (Day 1)'], ['API', 'Core calls, auth, pagination, errors, idempotency?', 'REST/gRPC, cursors, idempotency keys'], ['Data model', 'Entities, access patterns, keys, indexes, relational or not?', 'PostgreSQL default; KV/wide-column when justified'], ['Reads', 'How are hot reads served cheaply and close to users?', 'Cache, CDN, replicas, denormalization'], ['Writes', 'How do writes scale and stay safe?', 'Sharding, queues, batching, idempotency'], ['Async work', 'What can happen after the response?', 'Queues, workers, events, outbox'], ['Consistency', 'Which data must be strongly consistent and which not?', 'Leader per shard, quorums, eventual'], ['Failure', 'What if a node, zone, dependency or deploy fails?', 'Replication, failover, timeouts, breakers, retries with backoff'], ['Hot spots', 'Any skew: celebrities, viral items, sequential keys?', 'Key splitting, local caches, hybrid fan-out'], ['Security and privacy', 'Auth, authorization, data protection, abuse?', 'Rate limits, encryption, least privilege'], ['Observability', 'How will we know it is broken or slow?', 'Metrics, logs, traces, SLO alerts'], ['Cost and evolution', 'What is v1? What changes at 10x and 100x? What costs most?', 'Managed services, staged rollout']] } },
        { h: 'Good questions to ask the interviewer' },
        { ul: ['Who are the users and how many? Which features matter most?', 'What are the latency and availability expectations?', 'Is strong consistency required for any data?', 'Are we optimizing for read or write volume? Any traffic spikes (events, launches)?', 'Which regions? Any regulatory constraints (data residency, privacy)?', 'Is there an existing system or technology constraint?'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Four harder designs, outlined', short: 'Harder designs', min: 20, body: [
        "Use these outlines to test yourself: read the first line, close the lesson, design aloud, then compare.",
        { h: 'Ticketing (high contention)' },
        "**The challenge:** 500,000 people want 20,000 seats the second sales open; nobody may buy the same seat twice. **Approach:** put a **virtual waiting room** in front (a queue that admits users at a rate the backend can handle, with signed tokens). Event and seat-map pages are heavily cached (CDN), slightly stale is fine. **Seat hold:** a conditional atomic update `UPDATE seats SET status='held', held_by=?, hold_expires=now()+10min WHERE id=? AND status='available'` (or Redis `SET NX` with TTL for the hold, DB as truth); a background job releases expired holds. **Purchase:** idempotent payment, then flip to `sold` under a unique constraint on (event, seat). Shard by event; hot events get dedicated capacity. Add bot protection and per-user limits.",
        { h: 'Payments and wallet (money must be right)' },
        "**Principles:** strong consistency, auditability, idempotency. Model money as an **immutable double-entry ledger**: every transfer writes two entries (debit and credit) that sum to zero; balances are derived or maintained transactionally; never update in place. A payment is a **state machine** (created, authorized, captured, settled, failed, refunded). Calls to external processors are asynchronous with **idempotency keys**; a timeout means 'unknown', so query the processor by key rather than blindly retrying. Use the outbox for events, webhooks for results, nightly **reconciliation** against bank statements, and tokenization to keep card data out of scope. Shard by account; keep a single transaction within one shard.",
        { h: 'Ride hailing (real-time geo + matching)' },
        "Drivers stream locations every 4 s (about 100K writes/s) into an in-memory geo index sharded by region (S2/H3/geohash cells); riders request through the API; the **matching service** finds nearby available drivers, ranks by ETA, and sends an offer through the driver's WebSocket; an atomic claim (conditional update or Redis lock with TTL) ensures one rider per driver. **Trip state machine** (requested, matched, arrived, in progress, completed) stored durably; live location to the rider through pub/sub. ETA from a routing service with traffic data; surge pricing from per-cell supply/demand counters over a window; payment via the ledger design above.",
        { h: 'Google Docs (frontend and sync)' },
        "Document model as a tree of blocks with ranges for formatting; rendering by virtualized pages; edits as operations with **local echo**; server orders operations (OT) or CRDT merges; presence cursors as ephemeral messages; autosave via operation log plus snapshots; comments anchored to ranges that move as text changes; offline queue; version history; large documents split into chunks loaded on demand."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Keep going: a plan to retain and deepen', short: 'Retention plan', deep: true, body: [
        "Knowing this material for an interview is one goal. Becoming an engineer who understands systems from first principles is a longer one. Here is how to get there efficiently.",
        { h: 'How to retain' },
        { ul: ['**Retrieval beats rereading.** Use the Review tab daily. Recalling from memory strengthens it far more than reading again.', '**Spacing.** Reviewing at growing intervals (1, 3, 7, 16, 35 days) fixes memory with the least total time.', '**Explain it out loud** (the Feynman technique): teach a concept from the problem it solves, to someone with no background. Where you stumble is where you do not understand it yet.', '**Interleave.** Mix topics (a caching question, then a queue question) instead of drilling one at a time; it builds the skill of choosing the right tool.', '**Keep an error log:** every mistake in a quiz or mock, with the underlying principle. Review it weekly.', '**Ask why three times.** Why a cache? To cut reads. Why do reads matter? The database is the bottleneck. Why is it? One node, disk bound. The chain of causes is the understanding.'] },
        { h: 'Build things' },
        { ul: ['Implement a URL shortener with PostgreSQL and Redis, then load test it (k6, wrk) and watch where it breaks. Measure the hit ratio and the p99.', 'Write a toy consistent hash ring, an LRU cache, a token bucket, a small LSM-tree or write-ahead log, a mini Raft or a Bloom filter. Building a thing once teaches more than reading about it ten times.', 'Run a failure experiment: kill a database replica or a Redis node and observe what users would see.', 'Read **public postmortems** (AWS, Cloudflare, GitHub, Google). They are free case studies in how real failures chain together.'] },
        { h: 'Read, in this order' },
        { ol: ['*Designing Data-Intensive Applications* by Martin Kleppmann: the single best book for the ideas in Days 4 to 8.', 'The founding papers, one at a time: Dynamo, Bigtable, GFS, MapReduce, the Raft paper ("In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm"), the Kafka paper, Spanner.', 'The Google SRE book, for reliability, SLOs and incident practice.', 'For frontend: web.dev performance guides, the MDN documentation, and the source of one framework.'] },
        { h: 'A weekly rhythm' },
        "Review flashcards for 10 minutes daily. Once a week, pick one design, set a 45-minute timer, and talk it through out loud (record it). Compare with the outline, note gaps, and add them to your error log. Every month, read one paper or one postmortem and write a one-page summary in your own words."
      ]
    }
  ],
  lab: { title: 'Timed mock interview', min: 45, body: ["Pick one prompt at random (or ask a friend to pick), start the timer, and speak your design out loud, ideally recorded. Do not pause the timer to look things up.", { ul: ['**Backend prompts:** WhatsApp, a ticketing system, a news feed, YouTube, a payment system, Uber, a rate limiter, a web crawler, a distributed key-value store.', '**Frontend prompts:** autocomplete, an infinite photo feed, a chat UI, a Kanban board, a collaborative document, a video player, an image gallery, a spreadsheet.'] }, { widget: 'mocktimer' }, "Afterwards write down: three things you did well, three gaps, and one concept to relearn. Add the gaps to your error log, and use the Review tab."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: 'You realize mid-interview that a number you computed earlier was wrong. Best move?', opts: ['Hope nobody noticed', 'State the mistake, correct it and carry the consequence into the design', 'Restart from scratch silently', 'Blame the question'], a: 1, why: 'Self-correction shows rigor. Hiding the error is worse than the error.' },
    { q: 'A ticketing system sees 500K users for 20K seats at on-sale time. Which first line of defence protects the backend?', opts: ['A bigger database', 'A virtual waiting room that admits users at a controlled rate', 'Caching the seat counts forever', 'Disabling retries'], a: 1, why: 'Admission control smooths the stampede; the contended write path then handles manageable load.' },
    { q: 'Why is a money ledger modelled as immutable double-entry records?', opts: ['It is faster', 'Auditability and correctness: every movement is recorded and entries always balance', 'It avoids transactions', 'It saves storage'], a: 1, why: 'Append-only balanced entries give a complete audit trail and make errors detectable and reconcilable.' },
    { q: 'A call to the payment processor times out. What is the correct handling?', opts: ['Assume it failed and retry with a new request', 'Treat the outcome as unknown; retry with the same idempotency key or query the status', 'Assume it succeeded', 'Charge twice to be safe'], a: 1, why: 'A timeout hides whether the processor acted. Idempotency keys make retries safe, or query by key.' },
    { q: 'Which habit best builds long-term retention?', opts: ['Rereading notes the night before', 'Spaced retrieval practice and explaining concepts aloud', 'Highlighting', 'Watching more videos'], a: 1, why: 'Active recall spaced over time is the most reliable way to move knowledge into long-term memory.' },
    { q: 'A strong design answer adds a component when:', opts: ['It is popular', 'You can name the bottleneck it removes and its cost', 'The interviewer mentions it', 'It is open source'], a: 1, why: 'Every box needs a reason and a price. That is the discipline from Day 1.' }
  ],
  practice: {
    min: 10,
    prompt: ["**Teach it back.** Choose the concept from Days 1 to 13 you feel least sure about (for example consistent hashing, isolation levels, Raft, cache stampedes, hydration, CRDTs).", "Explain it as if to a smart new teammate who has never seen it. Start with the problem it solves, then how it works step by step with a concrete example, then the trade-offs and what goes wrong, and finally when you would not use it. Aim for 200 to 300 words. Then list the one question you could not answer."],
    hints: ['If you cannot explain the problem first, you do not yet understand the solution.', 'Use a concrete example with numbers.', 'Say what breaks: failure modes are where understanding shows.'],
    outline: [
      "A good teach-back has four parts: **the problem** (what hurts without this), **the mechanism** (how it works, with a small example), **the trade-offs** (what it costs and what it gives up), and **the limits** (when not to use it and how it fails). If any part was thin, reread the lesson and try again tomorrow, then add the card to your Review deck."
    ],
    rubric: 'Judge the explanation for: starting from the problem, accurate step-by-step mechanism with a concrete example, honest trade-offs and failure modes, clear limits and alternatives, and plain language without unexplained jargon. Point out any inaccuracy and what the learner should reread.'
  },
  cheat: ['Drive, think aloud, start simple, quantify, name trade-offs, talk failure, correct yourself openly.', 'Checklist: requirements, scale, API, data, reads, writes, async, consistency, failure, hot spots, security, observability, cost and evolution.', 'Ticketing: waiting room + atomic seat hold with expiry + idempotent payment. Payments: double-entry ledger, idempotency keys, unknown outcome on timeout, reconciliation.', 'Retain with retrieval, spacing, explaining aloud, interleaving and an error log. Build small versions of the systems.', 'Every component: what bottleneck does it remove, what does it cost?'],
  cards: [
    ['Give the master checklist order for a design.', 'Requirements, scale, API, data model, reads, writes, async work, consistency, failure, hot spots, security, observability, cost and evolution.'],
    ['How do you handle a seat-selling stampede?', 'Virtual waiting room for admission control, cached read pages, atomic seat hold with expiry, idempotent payment, unique constraint on sold seats.'],
    ['What is a double-entry ledger?', 'Immutable records where each transfer has equal debit and credit entries, giving audit and correctness.'],
    ['What do you do when a payment call times out?', 'Treat as unknown; retry with the same idempotency key or query status; reconcile later.'],
    ['What do interviewers look for beyond the correct design?', 'Driving the conversation, quantification, trade-off reasoning, failure analysis, humility and self-correction.'],
    ['What is the Feynman technique?', 'Explain a concept in simple words from the problem upward; gaps in your explanation reveal gaps in understanding.'],
    ['Why use spaced repetition?', 'Reviewing right before forgetting, at growing intervals, builds durable memory with the least time.'],
    ['What should you do after a failed mock?', 'Log the gaps and principles missed, relearn them, and re-attempt a similar design a few days later.']
  ]
}
);
